#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110377 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110744 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1f6f3cc2-e0b5-5263-896d-53369438a2f8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Midpoint Using the Midpoint Formula"}] :question/id #uuid "f46e0c53-c110-5990-b3ea-5dd27991cdc9" :question/math-academy-id "e-2557" :question/problem "Which number corresponds to the midpoint of the line segment $\\overline{AB}$ shown below?\n\n![](images/88/8850f207b51c6789073a0fb83b221a93290ade40b3f148248cac803fc1486452.png)" :question/worked-solution "The point $A$ corresponds to the number $x_1=-1$ while $B$ corresponds to $x_2=5$.\n\nTo find the number $x_m$ corresponding to the midpoint $M$, we use the midpoint formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{m} &= \\frac{x_{1} + x_{2}}{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{-1 + 5}{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{4}{2} \\\\\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the midpoint $M$ corresponds to $x_m=2$.\n\n![](images/07/07d9c940fbe2e5a14c5dc3fc1b46add8947f9ad0df7151f8693793cf70359688.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110378 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110745 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fb88880e-8b94-5223-b3fb-a4d80e795a00" :knowledge-point/title "Using Properties of Midpoints to Calculate an Unknown Value From a Diagram"}] :question/id #uuid "7c337d65-54d7-5264-b750-1a7f7bcd07f4" :question/math-academy-id "e-2558" :question/problem "Given that $M$ is the midpoint of $\\overline{PQ}$, what is the value of $x$?\n\n![](images/bf/bf08731ef8b88b55c1b3df41e58443beefb5e1a69b46757fbfb18c22d6c0b6b1.png)" :question/worked-solution "Since $M$ is the midpoint of $\\overline{PQ}$, the segment $\\overline{PQ}$ is twice the length of the segment $\\overline{PM}$. So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2PM &= PQ \\\\\n2(4x - 3) &= 5x + 9 \\\\\n8x - 6 &= 5x + 9\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we subtract $5x$ from both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n8x - 6 - 5x &= 5x + 9 - 5x \\\\\n3x - 6 &= 9\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we add $6$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3x - 6 + 6 &= 9 + 6 \\\\\n3x &= 15\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide both sides by $3{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{3x}{3} &= \\frac{15}{3} \\\\\nx &= 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110379 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110746 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4a4cad5-d0b0-51cb-921f-31a96e8e5eda" :knowledge-point/title "Using Properties of Midpoints to Calculate an Unknown Value From a Description"}] :question/id #uuid "6e321a78-087f-5962-a88c-e61bdc10de9f" :question/math-academy-id "e-2559" :question/problem "The point $M$ is the midpoint of the line segment $\\overline{LN}$. What is the value of $x$ if $MN=4x+1$ centimeters and $LM=3x+4$ centimeters?" :question/worked-solution "We have the following diagram.\n\n![](images/60/60e50f27aef61d5a97f9165dab922fa25cd80c114a0b11bbfbcdfe4c0fdf90e9.png)\n\nSince $M$ is the midpoint of $\\overline{LN}$, the two segments $\\overline{LM}$ and $\\overline{MN}$ have the same length. So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nLM &= MN \\\\\n3x + 4 &= 4x + 1 \\\\\n3x + 4 - 3x &= 4x + 1 - 3x \\\\\n4 &= x + 1 \\\\\n4 - 1 &= x + 1 - 1 \\\\\n3 &= x \\\\\nx &= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nx = 3\\,\\textrm{cm}\n$$\n\n."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110380 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110747 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c451d750-c40f-5b3f-9cd2-9eec197809de" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Midpoint Formula to Calculate an Endpoint"}] :question/id #uuid "c79d8054-b4f5-57e3-b816-5ac07ccfa90d" :question/math-academy-id "e-2560" :question/problem "For the diagram depicted below, which number corresponds to the point $R$ if $M$ is the midpoint of the line segment $\\overline{RS}$?\n\n![](images/d4/d4b49c7832633378a281295850be72f2ff664475b79f9b692591411c2d9ed6c4.png)" :question/worked-solution "From the picture, we can see that the point $S$ corresponds to the number $x_2=3$ while the midpoint $M$ corresponds to $x_m=1$.\n\nTo find the number $x_1$ corresponding to the point $R$, we first set up an equation using the midpoint formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{m} &= \\frac{x_{1} + x_{2}}{2} \\\\\n1 &= \\frac{x_{1} + 3}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $x_1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n1 &= \\frac{x_{1} + 3}{2} \\\\\n2 \\cdot 1 &= x_{1} + 3 \\\\\n2 &= x_{1} + 3 \\\\\n2 - 3 &= x_{1} \\\\\n- 1 &= x_{1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $R$ corresponds to $x_1=-1$.\n\n![](images/f4/f4897cf174e575e4999ba9605c9ddd03b3820c63fd77a1d7f87fdcaac171e35b.png)"}]}
